The “State of the European Territory” (SoET) report should provide observations of the main territorial development trends in Europe and offer policy advice to support the framing of integrated territorial development strategies and related implementation tools at the national, regional and local scale for the post-2020 period. Policy recommendations presented by the report should take full account of the European Commission’s multiannual financial framework and the related legislative proposals for the post-2020 period.

ESPON has a multitude of new territorial evidence from research activities covering not only diverse topics such as green infrastructures and ecosystem services, financial instruments, refugee flows, youth unemployment, cultural heritage, eHealth; these activities also look at various geographical areas from the entire EU territory plus Iceland, Liechtenstein, Norway, and Switzerland, to cross-border and larger functional regions. This evidence should be the key source for compiling the “State of the European Territory” report. Other relevant sources will be used to complement wherever information provided by ESPON might not be sufficient.

The report will cover the entire ESPON space at different geographical levels (EU, national, macro-regional, regional, local). If relevant, it can also address other territories such as the EU candidate countries or even a broader scale (e.g. FDI flows towards Europe). The exact thematic scope of the report will be further specified in cooperation with stakeholders and agreed upon between the ESPON EGTC and the service provider at the stage of the inception report. Discussions with DG REGIO and other DGs of the European Commission will influence the scope, as one of the goals of the report is to contribute to the preparation of the 8th Cohesion Report, which is to be published in 

2020. Therefore, the service provider should engage with ESPON’s main target group, i.e. policymakers. Several consultation rounds with the members of the ESPON Monitoring Committee (MC) are an integral element of the compilation process. The service provider will attend MC meetings/ESPON events to ensure that the report will be relevant for policymakers at the national level. By the same token, regional and local stakeholders are consulted and involved in the drafting process to ensure that the final publication will be of utmost use to them. A certain degree of consultation with the service providers who generated the original evidence that will feed the report is planned in to ensure that their results are correctly taken up.

The final “State of the European Territory” report will offer practical advice to national, regional and local governance levels to help the responsible authorities and stakeholders to design and implement their integrated territorial development strategies efficiently, applying different types of tools, with a view to achieving EU level and global priorities (e.g. SDG).

This service contract should provide inputs for the following key relevant policy processes:
Cohesion Report to be published by DG REGIO in 2020;
National policies on regional and spatial development;
Integrated territorial development strategies of cities and regions;
Renewal of the Territorial Agenda 2020;
Implementation of the Urban Agenda for the EU.
The “State of the European Territory” report will be published prior to a major ESPON conference in the second half of 2019.

The report is a co-production between the selected service provider and the ESPON EGTC. The ESPON EGTC provides the overall coordination of the compilation process, offering ESPON data and content input for each chapter based on ongoing research activities.

The current project "Urban greening for healthy breathing" will provide ecological, economic and social benefits in the cross-border region of Kyustendil - Kumanovo through the introduction of nature-friendly solutions, which helps us understand the advantages provided by nature to human society and mobilize investments to maintain and increasing these benefits. In other words, the green infrastructure that is set to be implemented under the project will provide sustainable ecosystem services that will contribute to human well-being and quality of life.
The project is aimed at achieving sustainable development of the green infrastructure of the Kyustendil - Kumanovo region, which would achieve synchrony and synergy with the other elements of the environment. The project partners developed this proposal with the aim of effective management and development of green infrastructure, which is achievable with consistent institutional measures, professionally implemented, with public involvement, with the application of modern technological approaches and reasonable consumption of resources to improve living standards. 
The main goal of the project is: Achieving a sustainable functional connection of the natural and planned elements of the green system in the urbanized cross-border environment Kyustendil - Kumanovo, so that the functioning of the green infrastructure provides multiple benefits for people and continuously improves the quality of life while preserving and development of the natural environment.
Specific objectives of the project:
1) Ensuring environmental, economic and social benefits in the Kyustendil - Kumanovo cross-border region through the introduction of environmentally friendly solutions in green infrastructure;
2) Preservation, storage and increase of natural capital, as well as protection of the health and well-being of citizens in the cross-border region;
3) Elaboration of joint green network solutions.
The implementation of the project will be carried out through the following approaches:
- Development of green infrastructure with sustainable use of resources and environmental protection;
- Development of green infrastructure in synergy with the priorities of interregional cross-border development and in accordance with European policies;
- Construction of green infrastructure in Kyustendil region and Kumanovo region through economically efficient and environmentally friendly solutions;
- Quality maintenance and sustainable development of the green infrastructure with the application of innovative solutions;
- Effectiveness and efficiency of green infrastructure management.

The aim of the PREMETER project is to prepare a main project (DEMETER) in the field of green modernisation of the Danube fleet. In accordance with the rules and standards in the Call Announcement and Programme Manual, the PREMETER project contains the following activities: 

1. Making a state of play report in the field of short-distance transport segment of Danube shipping; 

2. Preparing the detailed work plan of the DEMETER project; 

3. Mapping the funding possibilities to finance DEMETER project implementation; 

4. Researching socio-economic and environmental aspects of the DEMETER project in order to specify the intervention area; 

5. Building the possible partnership and disseminating the objectives and results. The total budget of the SMF project is 124.250,00 EUR and the implementation period is 12 months.

PREMETER will be implemented by a partnership formed by three project partners: Lake Velencei Development Nonprofit Limited Company from Hungary as Lead Partner 1, Pro Danube Management GmbH from Austria as Project Partner 2 and Faculty of Technical Sciences from Serbia as Project Partner 

3. Each partner has project experience not only in the field of transnational cooperation but also in relation with green and sustainable water transport. Lead Partner has successfully started a project which aim to realise a boat transformation from diesel to electric in lake environment. LP1 has significant experience in water transport and fleet management as well. Project Partner 2 is also involved in projects like GRENDEL and Green Danube, which projects aim to achieve a greener and sustainable Danube shipping. And also the experience and academic excellence of Project Partner 3 will be essential for the proper professional preparation and the precise definition of the intervention area of DEMETER project. The outputs will be managed by the professional leaders and experts within the partnership covered by the staff costs. LP1 will coordinate the preparation of the main project work plan and fund mapping, PP2 will manage the report on state of play and PP3 will implement the research activities, while each partner will be involved in the partnership building and communication.

Referring to policy documents there is an urgent need and at the same time significant potential for the inland waterway transport sector to reduce greenhouse gas and air pollutant emissions, in particular with regard to existing vessels. But although the shipping community is committed to achieving zero emission by 2050, actually there is no single matured technology that could guarantee this achievement. The efficiency and effectiveness of the different greening technologies strongly depend on the operational profile and operation area of the vessels involved. The suggestion of the partners who initiated the main project named DEMETER is that electrification is one of the optimal options among others for greening the Danube fleet for short-distance passenger/cargo transport. Therefore the main objective of DEMETER project is to contribute to the greening of the short-distance Danube fleet with electric transformation or retrofitting in order to reduce emissions. DEMETER contains activities like creating a professional platform to promote knowledge transfer, good practice sharing and policy making; implementing a pilot project which aims to prepare and realise a short-distance vessel transformation from diesel to electric; carrying out research and create an investment- and transformation planning methodology and tool (DE-meter).

As the main character, Demeter in Jules Verne's novel The Danube Pilot, the main project (DEMETER) also aims to “sailing along the entire length of the Danube”, more specifically to have spill-over impacts across the entire programme area on the long term. Thus, the estimated geographical scope of DEMETER project could be the entire Danube River basin, but the primary and direct partners involved would include the stakeholders from the cooperating countries, as well as regional and international organisations with interests in inland water passenger/cargo transportation, water tourism, environmental conservation and economic development in the region.

Both the SMF project and the main project will support the implementation of the relevant strategies. For example the European Union Strategy for Danube Region action plan is focusing on the waterway mobility with an action (Action 3) in the field of facilitate the Danube fleet modernisation. The objective of Action 3 is to develop the Danube fleet in order to reduce emissions of greenhouse gas and air and water pollutants with due regard to the objective of climate neutrality, and to introduce innovation in this sector. The projects will support the implementation of European Green Deal, Territorial Agenda 2030, Inland Waterway Transport Agenda for Europe 2021-2027 and Sustainable and Smart Mobility Strategy as well.

The Programme document, Territorial Strategy and Strategy Analysis, have thoroughly identified the weaknesses and challenges the cross-border area is facing related to tourism development.
Throughout the previous programming periods many projects were supported and thus improved the tourist infrastructure on both sides of the border. Besides the financed infrastructure works, a fair percentage of activities were aimed at improving the capabilities of the local population in topics related to tourism, as well as plentiful documents were elaborated in which the situation of the target area was analysed, and action plans and solutions were offered to improve the starting point. But none of these were focusing on the cross-border region as a target.
The project proposal will add value to the current situation through improvement with minimal interventions and the creation of several thematic tourist packages that will be appealing to every future visitor. The creation of these packages will help to overcome the undeveloped tourist product as a whole, and finally will produce a sustainable network of institutions and companies for services and offers in tourism. Furthermore, the project will propose adequate investments for improving the quality, labour market relevance, and inclusiveness of education and training, hence addressing income gap, the relatively high poverty risk, social exclusion and inequalities. This project will implement several different activities that will address these identified weaknesses and challenges and contribute in decreasing their impact, strengthening the already established networks, adding value to the existing tourist offers, and jointly develop one cross-border all-season tourist offer which will be piloted at the end of the project.
Tourism is a vital economic sector for both Bulgaria and North Macedonia. By collaborating and promoting joint tourism offerings, they can attract more international visitors and increase tourist spending. This influx of foreign currency can stimulate local economies, create jobs, and contribute to overall economic growth. Cross-border cooperation allows the partners to pool their resources, knowledge, and expertise, maximizing the potential of the shared assets for joint tourism development. This collaborative approach can lead to a more diverse and appealing tourism offering, attracting a broader range of visitors and generating more significant economic benefits. By collaborating across borders, the regions will leverage their individual strengths to create a more comprehensive and attractive tourism product, complement each other's tourism products in terms of unique attractions, cultural experiences, and historical sites that, when combined, create diverse and attractive all-season tour packages for tourists, which will extend tourists' stays and encourage them to explore both countries. With all project partner organizations working together, the target cross-border region will become a more competitive tourism destination in the international market. create a stronger presence and attract a larger share of global tourists who might be interested in visiting the cross-border region.
To overcome the challenges of the cross-border area, the project will address the local and regional authorities, SMEs, NGOs, local population, and the tourists as target groups, and implement several activities:
- beside the info days for presenting the expected outcomes, and for publishing the project’s results, 2 other events will be organized to promote the infrastructure works supported, and to comply with the Youth horizontal principle. Also, a tour with interested travel agencies will be organized to pilot the developed all-season integrated tourist offer.
- small-scale investments that will contribute to improve the cultural experience, enhance the potential of natural resources, and upscale the tourist attractions.
- development of a digital smart solution for self-guided tour and a video for better visual presentation of the all-season integrated tourist offer;
- create visual identities and marketing management plan for sustainable development of the tourist offer and the regional tourist destination;
- upscaling the human resources capacity in market economy, digital literacy and technology in tourism, and tourism destination management and certification.
At the end of the implementation period, the project will make the following results:
- reduced seasonality and create a steady flow of tourists;
- institutional collaboration for achieving common goals;
- enhanced the region's attractiveness to tourists and provide better amenities and experiences for visitors;
- better trained tourism professionals;
- opened possibilities for new job creation through development of (complementary) tourist services;
- long-term preservation of the area's assets;
- integration of smart solutions that enhances the overall tourist experience.

The rapid development of artificial intelligence (AI) has led to a transformation in various aspects of life, which inevitably reflected the drastic growth in the number of users of the new phenomenon. The widespread access and use of AI enables enormous benefits, but also raises significant challenges. One way for AI users to address these challenges is to develop AI literacy, which refers to human skills in various AI subject areas that enable purposeful, effective, and ethical use of AI technologies.
Generative artificial intelligence has made the public look at burning issues ranging from data privacy to plagiarism. World practices show that AI-generated texts are often filled with factual errors and images created by generative AI tools or used to spread misinformation. And children and adolescents remain the most vulnerable, especially from minority and marginalized groups.
In the 21st century, libraries are essential transformative institutions and identify the challenges facing today's communities and develop new tools and techniques to help better outcomes in education, workforce and economic development, digital and social justice. Driven by our desire to improve and streamline our services by introducing AI into our daily work, as well as to show that through the power of science and new technologies every child can be a creator, we developed a joint project with our long-term partners from Alexandru & Aristia Aman Dolj County Library in Craiova, Romania, for introducing AI in our daily work, as well as providing librarians and users with free access to new knowledge and skills.
The project "Creativity and artificial intelligence in the libraries of Vratsa and Dolj" will change the quality of work of both public institutions, through the introduction of a Virtual Assistant, and through the training of librarians, information specialists in the border region will be given the opportunity to be on the the high level expected by their users. Within the framework of the project, it is planned to implement the creative academy for children to work with AI, which will be organized by the two libraries and will be aimed at participants from 9 to 15 years old in the border area. It will also give a glimpse of such advanced AI in everyday life and help develop their creativity in a new and modern way.
Focusing on these main roles of public libraries, we developed a project in two main directions: Increasing the knowledge and skills of librarians in the field of artificial intelligence and Inspiring the creative mind of adolescents through artificial intelligence.
They will be implemented in the following activities:
Training of librarians from Vratsa and Dolj and implementing a digital assistant in service;
Equipment for training rooms;
Awareness activities among target groups and stakeholders;
Fostering creativity and improving the skills of working with artificial intelligence of adolescents;
Dissemination of the training results of the adolescents;
Providing opportunities for equal access to training.
Target groups include:
- Librarians and information specialists from the public libraries in the two border regions - Dolj region and Vratsa region;
- users of the two public libraries in Craiova and Vratsa;
- students aged 9 to 15, including children from minority groups;
- the local community.
Expected results:
Getting to know the details of this new phenomenon for the information society – Artificial Intelligence – sheds light on the most promising research opportunities for preparing AI users for an AI-driven future of work and society. The courses conducted will contribute to the economic development of the two areas, through the training of librarians in the two border areas, who in turn will pass on their experience to the local communities.
The Children's Education Academy will encourage curiosity and exploration in the fields of music, art, writing and animation. Developing children's imaginations as they create, innovate and explore the wonders of art and technology combined. Every child becomes a creator!
The sustainability of the project will be guaranteed by multiplying the results and the effect of the implemented activities. The implemented innovations and modernization of the official sites of the two public libraries, in Vratsa and Dolj, will provide an opportunity to improve the service of readers and the use of the virtual assistant 24/

7.
The software and equipment that will be purchased during the project will be used for future training and will be freely accessible to all registered readers of both libraries.

EnEfVin is about development and implementation of a cross border business model of collaboration of two SMEs, performing their economic activities in rural areas, for energy efficiency and reducing greenhouse gas emissions. 

It is expected to CONTRIBUTE TO THE PROGRAM'S GOAL - "Strengthening territorial cohesion" through joint and coordinated implementation by both partners of a new innovative business model for investments in energy efficiency and thus CONTRIBUTING TO THE EU PO 2: "A greener, low-carbon transition towards a net-zero carbon economy and a sustainable Europe by promoting a clean and fair energy transition..." 

The TERRITORIAL CHALLENGES identified in the program area (ongoing and still unresolved), which EnEfVin addresses are listed below: 

- WEAK EMPLOYMENT SKILLS – by preserving the jobs engaging individuals with higher skills levels in the wine industry; 

- LOW INCOMES – by preserved the jobs providing salaries over the average the region and economic sector; 

- UNEVEN ECONOMIC DEVELOPMENT IN THE CB AREA - By creating cross border business EnEfVin partnership is connecting a small town (Sungurlare) with a village near Kirklareli (Karakoc), which makes it possible to transfer experience in rural areas and to apply the good achievements of the partners in both places in the cross border region, creating added value. 

- LACK OF IDENTIFIED COMPANIES TO BE DRIVERS OF CHANGE in the cross-border area, which the Guidelines consider as one of the ways for innovation - Through WP3 project partners gain skills and experience in the implementation of innovative investment business model for energy efficiency which could serve as driver for change on local level. 

- EQUAL ACCESS OF PROJECT PARTNERS /BOTH SMES/ TO PROGRAM RESOURCES proving that enterprises are the change boosters - most concretely in the sector of vine production which is a traditional economic activity for the cross border area from centures; 

- NEED FOR MORE CROSS-BORDER COORDINATED ACTIONS IN THE ENERGY AND ENVIRONMENT AREA – by the well-structured, targeted, and formalized project partnership to create a model of cross border partnership for energy saving investments and to lower the carbon footprint, which have favorable environmental impacts. This new cross border business model of cooperation for energy efficiency is considered as a stage in their clean and fair energy transition to a net zero carbon economy, thereby contributing to the EU accession process and strengthening territorial cohesion. 

EnEfVin project is also planning activities in WP3 to improve the exchange of know-how, best practices and information between businesses on both sides of the border in the field of energy efficiency and reduction of greenhouse gas emissions. 

The EXPECTED IMPACT that the project will make through investments in energy efficiency can be described by: 


1. Improving the level of energy efficiency of both project partners; 


2. Reducing the amount of greenhouse gases from the activities of both partners; 


3. Contribution to the understanding of energy savings efforts and transition to a low-carbon economy by small businesses in rural areas. 

The MAIN RESULTS that EnEfVin (both PPs) will deliver are: 
1 Energy savings of annual primary energy consumption, as a result of energy savings measures over 30 % (2 x 30 %); 


2. Reduced amount of CO2 emissions (Environmental Benefits), expected over 21 t CO2 eq./year; 


3. Improved perception of the transition to clean energy and low-carbon transition by local SMEs - participants in project initiatives, regarding the energy efficiency policy of Bulgaria and Turkey and shared new and innovative wine production practices. 

The MAIN OUTPUTS are: 


1. Average value of the energy savings of the annual primary energy consumption for the whole project is expected to be over >30%/year. per PP;


2. Confirmed effect of each of the energy saving measures of both PPs of at least 5% energy savings. 


3. Reduced amount of CO2 emissions is expected to be over 21 t CO2 eq./year per project; 


4. 40 SMEs from both sides of the border, participating in a seminars on "EnEfVin – "How wine production is changing in the new realities of climate change" and "To be more competitive and socially responsible through energy efficiency investments". 

EnEfVin provides benefits not only for Project Partners, but for SMEs participants in the seminars, SMEs reached by project communication channels; local communities; local and national public authorities in the cross border area.

The action is applying CROSS BORDER APPROACH to address identified common needs and problems of project partners, providing an innovative cross border solution for better performance based on energy efficiency. 

EnEfVin IMPLEMENTATION APPROACH is project-based to achieve common goals, with defined resources for a fixed period of time.

GREEN FOOD is about development and implementation of an innovative business model for collaboration of two SMEs in the cross-border region for energy efficiency and reducing greenhouse gas emissions.

The model represents a PARTNERSHIP FOR COOPERATION AND TRANSFER OF BEST PRACTICES in the energy sector developed by National Association of Small and Medium Business under the "Green Energy" project (BG161PO001/

4.2-01/2008/035-U-03). Characteristics of the model that are further developed and applied in our project are:


1. INTERACTION OF THE THREE SECTORS OF SOCIETY: BUSINESS, NGO AND PUBLIC AUTHORITY.
BUSINESS is the beneficiary;
PUBLIC AUTHORITIES (local municipalities and the Consulate General of Bulgaria in Edirne) significantly assisted in the preparation process,
The NGO (employer's organization NASMB) plays the role of an intermediary who:
٠identifies the interested local companies to which the experience will be transferred;
٠helps establish contact and develop collaborative partnerships;
٠in understanding and adapting progressive practices and techniques, innovatively applies benchmarking analysis, not only as a comparative analysis, but as a process of continuous comparison and approximation to the best practices.



2. The exchange of know-how and best practices is carried out through INTER-REGIONAL (in this project – through TRANSBORDER) COOPERATION, because of common territorial challenges:
٠ specific local problems, needs insistently require implementation of specific solutions;
٠ complex topic requires a wide range of know-how and best practices
٠ subject matter expertise in the target region is scarce.

The TERRITORIAL CHALLENGES identified in the program area (ongoing and still unresolved), which GREEN FOOD addresses are listed below: 
- weak employment skills,
- low incomes; 
-lack of identified companies to be drivers of change in the cross-border area;
- equal access of project partners /both SMEs/ to program resources proving that enterprises are the change boosters; 
- need for more cross-border coordinated actions in the energy and environment area.

A well-structured, targeted, fruitful, and formalized project partnership is being built to apply an innovative cross-border business model of cross-border partnership for coordinated actions in the field of energy efficiency, with energy savings. Partnerships connects a small town (Kırklareli) with a large metropolis (Burgas), which makes it possible to transfer experience from one place to the other and to apply the good achievements of the partners in both places in the cross-border region, creating added value.

Joint investments to reduce greenhouse emissions and energy consumption are a stage in PPs clean and fair energy transition to a net zero carbon economy in the food sector, thereby contributing to the EU accession process and strengthening territorial cohesion.

It is expected to CONTRIBUTE TO THE PROGRAM'S GOAL - "Strengthening territorial cohesion" and thus CONTRIBUTING TO THE EU PO 2: "A greener, low-carbon transition..."

All above listed achievements will be used by PPs, SME participants in the seminar, SMEs in business contacts with partners, local communities, local and national public authorities, for which they are planned additional supporting measures.

The EXPECTED CHANGES (impact) that the project will make through investments in energy efficiency is:


1. Achieving a higher level of energy efficiency of PPs;


2. Reduction of the carbon intensity of the two enterprises;


3. Changes have occurred in the behavior, capacity and way of working of regional SMEs, which are beginning to more actively adopt the principles of energy efficiency and related production practices having in mind GREEN FOOD good practice.

The MAIN RESULTS that the project will produce are:
1 Energy savings of annual primary energy consumption, as a result of energy savings measures;


2. Reduced amount of CO2 emissions (Environmental Benefits), expected over 230 t CO2 eq./year per project;


3. Improved perception of the transition to a greener, low-carbon/net-zero carbon economy by regional SMEs, through applying the principles of energy efficiency and related production practices.

The MAIN RESULTS (outputs) that the project will produce are:


1. Average value of the energy savings of the annual primary energy consumption for the whole project with more than >30 kWh/year per PP.


2. Confirmed effect of each of the energy saving measures of each of the partners of at least 5% energy savings.


3. Reduced amount of CO2 emissions (Environmental Benefits), expected over 230 t CO2 eq./year per project;


4. 40 interested companies from both sides of the border, participating in two seminars.

The CROSS-BORDER APPROACH is necessary to address COMMON NEEDS of PPs both SMEs, providing an innovative cross-border solution for energy efficiency.

THE IMPLEMENTATION APPROACH is project-based, to achieve common goals, with specific resources for a specific time.

EnEfProducts is for business model of cooperation of one small and one medium-sized enterprises in the cross-border region /CBR/ for energy efficiency /EnEf/ and reduction of greenhouse gas emissions. It is expected to CONTRIBUTE TO THE PROGRAM OBJECTIVE - "Strengthening Territorial Cohesion" through the joint and coordinated implementation by both partners of a new innovative business model for investments in energy efficiency and thus CONTRIBUTE TO EU PO 2: "Greener, low-carbon transition to a zero-carbon economy and a sustainable Europe by promoting a clean and fair energy transition..."

TERRITORIAL CHALLENGES identified in the program area (ongoing and still unresolved) that EnEfProducts addresses are:

- LOW SKILLS FOR EMPLOYMENT – through retained/newly created jobs engaging individuals with higher skill levels in industrial production;

- LOW INCOME – through preserved/newly created jobs providing salaries higher than the average for the region and the economic sector;

- UNEVEN ECONOMIC DEVELOPMENT IN THE AREA OF THE CB - By creating cross-border /CB/ business partnerships connecting a small town (Kirklareli) with another small town (Kameno), which gives the opportunity to transfer experience from one place to another and apply the good achievements of PPs and in both locations in CBR, creating added value;

- LACK OF IDENTIFIED COMPANIES TO BE DRIVERS OF CHANGE in the cross-border area - Through WP3, PPs gain skills and experience in implementing an innovative EnEf investment business model that builds on best practice of “GREEN ENERGY” project, introducing a new good practice to follow in the CBR;

- EQUAL ACCESS OF THE PROJECT PARTNERS /THE TWO SMEs/ TO THE PROGRAM RESOURCES, proving that enterprises are the engines of change - most specifically in the industrial production sector, which is an important economic activity for the CBR;

- NEED FOR MORE CROSS-BORDER COORDINATED ACTIONS IN THE FIELD OF ENERGY AND THE ENVIRONMENT – Established partnership through the mediation of business organizations on both sides of the border: National Association of Small and Medium Businesses /NASMB/ of which BG Plast Ltd is a member and the Chamber of Commerce and Industry of Kirklareli, of which Bimeks Tel Ltd is a member, to implement a CB partnership model for coordinated actions in energy saving and favorable impact on the environment.

Through joint investments to reduce greenhouse gas emissions and energy consumption, the model of the “GREEN ENERGY” project, successfully established as a best practice by the NASMB, the Municipality of Brezovo and the Province of Bergamo, is being upgraded by the current project. PPs build on and innovate it as a CB cooperation business model for EnEf, being a stage in their clean and fair energy transition to a net-zero carbon economy in industrial production.

EnEfProducts also plans activities to improve the exchange of know-how, best practices and information between businesses on both sides of the border in the field of EnEf and the reduction of greenhouse gas emissions.

THE EXPECTED CHANGES (impact) that is:


1. Achieving a higher level of energy efficiency of the project partners;


2. Reduction of the carbon intensity of the two enterprises;


3. There have been changes in the behavior, capacity and way of working of regional SMEs, which are starting to more actively adopt the principles of EnEf and related production practices, bearing in mind the good practice of EnEfProducts.

The MAIN OUTPUTS are:
1 Energy savings from annual primary energy consumption, as a result of energy-saving measures;


2. Reduced amount of CO2 emissions (environmental benefits), expected over 150 t CO2 eq./year;


3. Improved perception of the transition to a greener, low-carbon transition to a net-zero carbon economy by the regional SMEs directly covered by the target group actors, in terms of EnEf principles and related production practices.

The MAIN RESULTS (results) that the project will produce are:


1. Average energy savings of the annual primary energy consumption for the whole project with more than >30 kWh/year.


2. Confirmed effect of each of the energy saving measures of each of the partners of a minimum of 5% energy saving.


3. Reduced amount of CO2 emissions (environmental benefits), expected over 150 t CO2 eq./year;


4. 40 interested companies from both sides of the border participating in a seminar on "Industrial production and sustainable development" and "The Fourth Industrial Revolution and SMEs".

