The CBC region Bulgaria - Turkey has great natural and cultural-historical potential for tourism development and especially on the territory of the Municipalities of Topolovgrad (Bulgaria) and Büyükkarştıran (Turkey). However, the future development of the tourism sector will not be possible without active engagement of adolescents and their education from an early age in green models for the preservation of the environment and development of sustainable tourism products and services.

The common territorial challenge that the project will cope with is the development of culture, perceptions and potential for sustainable development of tourism from an early age among the young population in Topolovgrad and Büyükkarştıran. Both municipalities as part of the cross-border region have the potential to attract tourists due to their cultural heritage and natural beauty, but currently face challenges related to appropriate infrastructure and activities for the promotion and implementation of green practices in tourism by the younger generation. Focusing on cultural initiatives and green practices, the project will contribute to the economic growth of the region and improve the quality of life of local communities, especially young people.

In order to tackle the identified challenges and needs, the Green Smile project sets the following main objective - to promote the development of the tourist potential on the territory of the municipalities of Topolovgrad (Bulgaria) and Buyukkarştaran (Turkey) by promoting cultural initiatives and green practices among the young population.
The results of the project will benefit the local community of young people in both municipalities by leading to positive changes in their perception of sustainable tourism and green models for the preservation of cultural and natural heritage in the CBC region.

The main outputs that the project will produced include:

- Established children's summer center for culture and art in Topolovgrad;
- Created Art Workshop for Traditional Crafts and Promotion of Cultural Heritage in Büyükkarştıran;
- Developed Strategy for Promoting Green Models among Children and Young People in the CBC Region;
- Developed marketing strategy for branding and promotion of Eco Festival "Green Smiles" as part of the regional tourist product;
- Organised ecological walks "In the embrace of the mountain and the sea";
- Organised 2 festivals "Green smiles" – 1 in Topolovgrad and 1 in Büyükkarştıran.

Both muncicipalities will benefit from the project by promoting sustainable tourism and cultural initiatives on their territory. The project will improve the perception of young people from both municipalities about sustainable tourism and green models for the preservation of cultural and natural heritage in the region.

The project uses a holistic approach to address the identified challenges and opportunities in the development of tourism in the program area by combining investment measures for the development of the necessary infrastructure and soft measures to engage the young generation in activities to increase the tourism potential of the cross-border region by encouraging and participating in green and creative initiatives. It goes beyond existing practices by promoting sustainable tourism and emphasizing cross-border cooperation among children and young people in both municipalities.

The project's approach goes beyond existing practice in the sector, placing special emphasis on sustainable tourism by engaging the youngest generation in the CBC region. The project focuses on building joint capacity among children and young people in the two municipalities from Bulgaria and Turkey for cooperation, exchange and green practices in the field of tourism and preservation of the natural and cultural-historical heritage there.

In the long run, the joint solution created within the project to engage the young generation in activities for sustainable development of tourism potential will become a working model for the development of the tourism sector and the preservation of cultural heritage and natural sites on the territory of the cross-border region, both on Bulgarian and Turkish sides.

DanubeCrafts respond to the common challenge of DRP - Specific Objective 

3.3, by addressing rich cultural heritage and unexploited potential of traditional crafts, customs, and rituals, some of them fading into obscurity, together with the untapped potential of thematic tourism in Danube region. The project aims to change this situation through a multi-stakeholder jointly approach, through the preservation and development of traditional craft products and services with the support of thematic tourism “instruments”. 
The overall objective of the project is to preserve, promote, and sustainably enhance traditional crafts along a “Traditional Crafts Route”, comprising several touristic itineraries (locations within local communities) with cultural and traditional attractions, to stimulate employment and broaden knowledge on generating new income streams for local craftsmen and artisans, with special attention to vulnerable groups and youth.
The main outputs will be as follows: 
- a joint strategy (O

1.1) to guide regional activities for sustainable utilization of traditional craftsmanship resources, including the development of a detailed route for traditional crafts and thematic tourism, ensuring its sustainability, in the benefit of craftsmen, local organizations, authorities, and tourism industry players, to support rural communities development, including positive implications for tourism sector;
- regional action plans (O

1.2), implementing traditional crafts workshops and demonstrations, participation in exhibition events and similar actions, addressing traditional professional groups (craftsmen, artisans, etc.) as potential employees/entrepreneurs, with the implication of entities active in thematic/cultural tourism;
- Methodology for Training Programs– InnoCraft Framework (O

2.1), focused on testing of a comprehensive methodology for training / workshop programs aimed at improving operational capacities in traditional crafts. The methodology serves as a foundation for future training initiatives, incorporating workshops, interactive learning tools, and a recognition framework. The testing validates its relevance and adaptability, providing the basis for a scalable and sustainable training model;
- Toolkit for enhancing operational capacities: Training/Workshops, InnoCraft-Innovative Ideas Collection and Honorary Certificate Award (O

2.2) will provide customized ideas & solutions for regional actors to offer new integrated crafts + touristic services and products, also providing content topics for the trainings for potential participants in the value chain of traditional crafts + tourism products / services development or as beneficiaries;
- "CraftBiz Impact" Kit (O

2.3) provides essential tools for impact analysis and business model development in the traditional crafts and thematic tourism industry, with comprehensive resources and practical guides that facilitates strategic planning and sustainable growth for professionals and entrepreneurs;
- Crafting Journeys: Unveiling Potential Itineraries for the Traditional Crafts Route (TCR) (O

2.4), including essential aspects such as the value of cultural crafts, monuments, sites, protected areas, as well as accessibility, available services, etc., with focus on slow thematic tourism, being brought to the public's attention through various communication channels, including social media, short storytelling videos etc.;

- Integration of Smartphone App across three Pilot Cases - Elevating Cultural Exploration on Traditional Crafts Route (O

3.1), identifying and piloting 3 itineraries to be integrated into the TCR smartphone application, featuring various traditional crafts topics, by participating of craftsmen in thematic fairs and exhibitions to showcase the itineraries and attract potential application users;
- Traditional Crafts Route (TCR) Certification (O

3.2), preparing and initiating the application process for obtaining certification as a European cultural route of traditional crafts, or even integration into existing routes, such as the "European Route of Ceramics".
The planned approach is transnational, with a widespread coverage of DR, i.e. project consortium, and organizations cooperating across borders, ensuring an extensive geographical coverage, and allowing the development of a pan-Danube strategical orientation.
DanubeCrafts brings an original and innovative approach, by offering a unique opportunity for the development of a slow and sustainable thematic tourism, focused on local assets and valuable knowledge, as a supportive “instrument” to the preservation and capitalization of the untapped traditional crafts sector in rural and remote communities of the DR.

Virgin forests are globally important for their biodiversity, protection from natural disasters, carbon storage, climate regulation, provision of clean water, etc. Most of virgin forests are located within the boundaries of protected areas. There are also virgin forests in the world, including the programme area that are not under any protection regime and therefore can be damaged. Conservation of virgin forests growing in the Black Sea basin area will contribute to the creation of a green belt of the Black Sea and therefore make the region greener and cleaner. 
The objective of the project is to enhance the protection and preservation of biodiversity and natural heritage through strengthened partnerships and joint action by universities in neighboring countries, involving relevant stakeholders in the Black Sea basin area to contribute to the creation of a green belt around the Black Sea; and the specific objective is to contribute to the protection and preservation of virgin forest stands of high conservation value in Artvin province and Adjara AR through partnership and the use of innovative approaches
The project will also raise the awareness of local communities and other stakeholders about the importance and means of conserving virgin forests and the benefits they provide, such as carbon sequestration, biodiversity conservation, protection from natural disasters, clean water, economic benefits, etc. through public awareness and education campaigns.
As the proposed project is multifaceted involving research, innovative technologies and activities at policy and grassroot levels, a strong partnership of organizations with different and complementary expertise is needed. Adjara and Artvin province belong to the Caucasus Ecoregion - one of the most biologically rich and culturally diverse regions on Earth and are characterized by similarities in terms of biodiversity, therefore the transnational approach is highly relevant. The project will be implemented through the partnership of ACU as a lead partner and BSU , AUTh and C&E as partners. Project partners will be involved in project related activities according to their competence and relevance: e.g., virgin forest stands in Artvin forests will be identified and mapped through field visits with participation of students of Artvin and Batumi universities, local foresters and Georgian, Turkish and Greek experts using the methodology developed by C&E; a research on virgin forests in Artvin will be developed jointly by three universities; a digital map will be developed by Greek experts with participation of Georgian and Turkish specialists; guidelines for the protection of virgin forest stands will be developed by AUTh with the involvement of international team of experts of the project and other relevant stakeholders on the basis on the best EU practices; public information campaign and curricula on conservation of virgin forest stands will be developed by ACU and BSU with the involvement of international team of experts of the project on the basis of recommendations from the AUTh. Promotional materials of the project will be developed and produced by ACU and C&E for respective countries.
All materials and outputs produced by the project will be widely communicated and advertised through professional networks, newsletters and social media, as well as placed in e-library of the involved universities, project web site, project partner’s websites as open source data to enable their replication and upscaling by relevant stakeholders. E.g.: 
-	the digital map of virgin forest stands in Artvin and Adjara will be freely accessible for forestry agencies, students and scientists, as well as other stakeholders. The map can be used for further research, planning, it can be updated to incorporate new data;
-	the report of the research of virgin forests in Artvin can be used as a basis for further research or decision-making on specific management interventions and/or planning; 
-	guidelines for the protection of virgin forest stands in Artvin and Adjara based on the best EU practices will be transferred to relevant forestry authorities for implementation, distributed among relevant forestry practitioners and educators and placed in internet;
-	curricula on protection and conservation of virgin forests will be introduced in Artvin and Batumi universities, as well as other educational institutions of the region.
-	information materials can be used in other regions/countries as they can be adapted to accommodate the needs and requirements of specific communities/countries;
-	information corners in Artvin and Batumi botanical gardens will be used as information sources for visitors and tourists.
To ensure the sustainability of project results, project partners will continue cooperation after project completion. Strengthened capacity and gained experience will allow them to enter into partnership with each other and other organizations to address new and challenging tasks.

The Lower Danube region faces serious ecological and social challenges that do not stop at national borders. Urbanisation, habitat degradation and the spread of invasive alien species (IAS) have reduced biodiversity, weakened ecological connectivity and limited the quality of life for citizens. Riverside areas in Tutrakan (BG) and Oltenita (RO) are degraded and underused, while local communities and schools have limited opportunities to access green spaces, environmental education and practical examples of biodiversity-friendly solutions. The common challenge addressed by this project is to restore and reconnect these degraded areas into a cross-border green corridor, while equipping local actors with the knowledge, tools and motivation to manage invasive species and protect native biodiversity.

The overall objective of the project is to restore and revitalise 9.17 hectares of degraded urban and riverside land (

3.4 ha in Tutrakan and 5.77 ha in Oltenita) and to establish a durable cross-border knowledge and education framework for biodiversity protection. The project will bring measurable change by creating accessible, multifunctional green infrastructure, by producing a joint scientific base and action plan for IAS management, and by equipping schools with educational tools that strengthen ecological awareness and citizen engagement. The expected change is a tangible improvement in the ecological, social and educational value of the riverfronts, together with stronger cross-border cooperation and shared responsibility for biodiversity protection.

As a result of the project activities the project will provide:

- A cross-border scientific study and Action Plan for invasive alien species (WP1);
- Pilot removal actions of IAS implemented with volunteers (WP1);
- 9.17 ha of restored and biodiverse green infrastructure in Tutrakan and Oltenita (WP2);
- Biodiversity-friendly facilities such as pollinator gardens, bird observatories, sensory alleys, educational trails and an outdoor amphitheatre (WP2);
- Educational outputs: smart cube game, bilingual species cards, electronic book, school lectures and awareness videos (WP3);
- Two conferences, one international roundtable and a set of stakeholder workshops (WP1 & WP3).


The main target groups are the citizens of Tutrakan and Oltenita, local schools (two in Tutrakan, five in Oltenita), NGOs, volunteers, municipal administrations and regional environmental authorities. Visitors and small businesses benefit from improved eco-tourism opportunities and higher attractiveness of the riverside areas.
The approach is based on joint action: research, investment and education are designed and implemented together by Bulgarian and Romanian partners. The cross-border dimension is essential because ecosystems, invasive species and riverside landscapes are shared, and solutions cannot be developed in isolation. Tutrakan and Oltenita are towns just across the Danube, and their cooperation creates a visible and replicable example of how local authorities and communities can work together on both ecological restoration and awareness-raising.

The novelty of the project lies in its integration of scientific knowledge, infrastructure investment and educational innovation. It is not limited to physical restoration but creates multifunctional spaces that combine biodiversity protection with recreation and learning. Tools such as the interactive smart cube game and sensory alleys make biodiversity tangible and engaging for young audiences. The project also introduces the concept of a “river school” and outdoor amphitheatre on the riverside, linking ecological content with cultural and social functions. By embedding biodiversity education in schools and community events, the project ensures that knowledge is transferred across generations and borders.

As a whole, the project delivers a comprehensive solution to a shared challenge in the Lower Danube: degraded riverside zones, declining biodiversity and limited ecological awareness. By combining ecological restoration, pilot IAS management and innovative education, it creates long-term value for citizens and institutions in both Bulgaria and Romania, while contributing to EU biodiversity and green infrastructure goals.
